      plan.md                   # Kept for reference
  Archive\
    <KEY>-###\
      <KEY>-###.md              # Older issues (30+ days)
```

## PlanningWorkspace

Location: `<workspace>\PlanningWorkspace\`

Contains full clones of the repos, always on main branch. Updated before planning phases.

**See `PlanningWorkspace\repos.md` for repo URLs and tech details.**

**Rules:**
- READ ONLY - never modify files
- Never switch branches
- Never commit or push

**Update command (run before planning):**
```
Bash(cd <workspace>/PlanningWorkspace && for dir in */; do (cd "$dir" && git fetch origin && git reset --hard
      origin/main); done)
```

## Issue File Format

**Agent's turn** (file starts with `go`):
```markdown
go
# [Issue Title]

Status: Planning | Plan Review | Development | Code Review | Development Complete | Complete
Tier: Trivial | Standard | Full
Priority: High | Medium | Low
Branch: [link]
PR: [link]
Blocked:
Jira: <Jira site>/browse/<KEY>-###
Jira Status: [verbatim Jira status name, refreshed by /status]

## Discussion
_(Newest first - format: [agent] message)_

[agent] message...

## Description
[From Jira]
```

**Not agent's turn** (file starts with `#`): identical, minus the leading `go` line.

**Field notes:**
- `Tier:` set during Phase 1 Triage. One of `Trivial` (skip spec + plan), `Standard` (skip spec, write plan), `Full` (write both). Locked once set unless explicitly retriaged.
- `go` line — present = available for the autonomous `/work` full pass. A targeted `/work <item>` clears it on claim (naming the item is consent); progression is then conversational. There is no separate lock field — `go`-absence is the only coordination signal.
- `Blocked:` empty when not blocked, contains reason when blocked.
- `Jira Status:` only on ticketed items (omit for adhoc).

The full template, priority mapping, and Jira-import procedure live in the plugin's `references/issue-file-format.md`.

## Two-Field Status Model

Local issue files have **two** status fields with very different ownership:

| Field | Owner | What it represents |
|-------|-------|--------------------|
| `Status:` | Claude / orchestrator / user (local) | Where the work is in the **local** workflow — drives what `/work` dispatches and what `/status` shows |
| `Jira Status:` | `/status` (read-only mirror of Jira) | The current Jira ticket status, refreshed every `/status` run |

**Why two fields:** "Code Review" locally means "agent finished, PR is up, awaiting human review." Jira's "Code Review" status is a separate, formal stage the user transitions manually after merging. These cadences naturally diverge — local progresses faster — and conflating them caused the local field to oscillate every sync run. The two fields are now decoupled.

**Rules:**
- Only `/status` writes `Jira Status:`. Nothing else touches it.
- `Status:` is owned by whoever is currently driving the work (planning agent, dev agent, user). `/work` reads it to decide what to dispatch but never overwrites it from Jira.
- The dashboard shows both side by side; mismatch is normal (e.g., local `Code Review`, Jira `In Development` while the user reviews the PR before transitioning Jira).
- Sweep (move to `Complete\`) is owned by `/status` Phase 3 — that skill is the sole authority for the trigger condition and performs the move. `/work` never sweeps. (Gist: an item is done when local `Status:` or the Jira mirror says so — but see `/status` for the exact rule rather than relying on this line.)

### `Tier:` field

`Tier:` lives alongside `Status:` but is owned independently: set once during Triage, it gates which phases run. There is no separate mode/lock field — the `go` flag's presence is the only coordination signal between the autonomous full pass and a targeted focused session.

### Local `Status:` values (the ones Claude/`/work` cares about)

| Status | Meaning | `/work` action |
|--------|---------|----------------|
| Planning | Claude researching/designing | Dispatch triage/planning per Tier |
| Plan Review | plan.md written, awaiting human approval | None until `go` re-added; then advance to Development |
| Development | Claude coding (branch exists) | Resume dev subagent |
| Code Review | PR created, waiting for human review | None when unflagged. **With `go` set:** read the file and act — address recorded PR feedback (resume dev), else report and ask. `go` is never ignored. |
| Development Complete | Local work done, awaiting Jira transition | None when unflagged. With `go` set: read the file and do any described follow-up, else report state. |
| Complete | Done locally — sweep eligible | Swept by `/status` (no dispatch) |

`Plan Review` and `Code Review` are deliberately distinct gates: re-adding `go` to a `Plan Review` item approves the plan and advances it to Development; `Code Review` means the PR awaits your review in Azure DevOps. **The `go` flag is always the work signal — a `go`-flagged item is never skipped on account of its status.**

`In Development` is also accepted as a synonym for `Development` (Jira's spelling). Files may carry `QA`, `QA Complete`, or `Deployed` as local statuses for parity; unflagged they sit idle, `go`-flagged they get read-and-acted-on like any other.

### `Jira Status:` values

Whatever Jira says — passed through verbatim, including casing (e.g. `In Development`, `Development Complete`, `Ready for QA Testing`). Phase 2 normalises by trimming only.

## Workflow Phases & Skill Sourcing

The Jira-driven workflow has phases (Setup → Triage → Spec → Plan → Execute → Review → Wrap). The exact mechanics — what each phase does, which superpowers skill it invokes, dispatch order, status transitions — live in the skill bodies themselves rather than being mirrored here. **The skill body is the source of truth; this template is the user-facing config.**

| Want to know... | Read the skill |
|---|---|
| Process `go`-flagged items, or work one named item as a focused in-chat session (`/work <key-or-hint>` — also imports a not-yet-local Jira key inline) | `/work` |
| Discovery + housekeeping pass (refresh PlanningWorkspace, sync Jira Status, sweep, dashboard, suggest next work) | `/status` |
| Workspace bootstrap, prereq check, scaffold flow | `/workspace-init` |
| Scaffolding unticketed work from a slug or free-form description | `/adhoc` |
| Local Playwright smoke walk against your dev branch | `/smoke` |
| End-to-end QA verification (dev/qa/staging) | `/qa` |
| New Story-workflow ticket (CRD, CO, etc.) | `/create-issue` |
| CAB ticket creation in the CHANGE project | `/create-change-issue` |
| Pre-PR self-review (build, code-reviewer, architect-review) | `/author-review` |
| Independent reviewer pass on someone else's PR | `/reviewer-check` |
| Delta re-review after author addresses prior feedback | `/rereview` |

This template intentionally does NOT duplicate phase-by-phase content. If `/work` or `/status` behaviour changes, the skill body changes — and this section keeps pointing at it instead of going stale.

## Git Conventions

**Branch:** `fb/<KEY>-###` (e.g. `fb/CO-322`, `fb/CRD-100`)

**Commits:** Always prefix with `[Claude]`
- Example: `[Claude] Add validation for user input`

**Before dev work:**
```bash
git fetch origin
git log HEAD..origin/main --oneline
git merge origin/main  # if updates exist
```

## No GitHub CLI

PCI hosts code on Azure DevOps on-prem. The GitHub CLI (`gh`) does not work against ADO and is **not used anywhere in this workflow**:

- **Opening PRs** is a manual step. After Phase 6 wrap pushes the branch, the user opens the PR in the Azure DevOps web UI. No skill calls `gh pr create`.
- **Fetching PR data** (diffs, descriptions, review comments) for `/author-review`, `/reviewer-check`, `/rereview`: ask the user to paste the diff, or check out the branch and run `git diff <target>...HEAD`. Do not suggest `gh pr diff`, `gh pr view`, or `gh api`.
- **`/workspace-init`** does not check for `gh` in its CLI prereq phase.

If a future workflow needs PR-tree introspection from a script, use the Azure DevOps REST API (or an ADO MCP if/when one is wired up) rather than reaching for `gh`.

## Local Smoke Testing

The `/smoke` skill runs an autonomous local walk-through of a Jira ticket's acceptance criteria. Use it to verify your own feature branch before opening a PR. Local environment only — for deployed envs use `/qa`. See the skill body for the full pipeline.

## QA Verification

The `/qa` command verifies a ticket's implementation end-to-end across the multi-app ecosystem (dev, qa, or staging). Hybrid execution: Claude drives automated steps via Chrome MCP, prompts for manual steps, validates assertions. See the skill body for the full pipeline.

## Adhoc Work

Work that doesn't have a Jira ticket — investigations, debugging spikes, cross-repo audits, unticketed bug fixes — uses the same shape as ticketed work but with a kebab-case slug instead of a Jira key.

**Create:** `/adhoc <slug> "<title>"` — scaffolds `Active\<slug>\<slug>.md` with the standard issue shape and `go` flag set.

**Differences from ticketed work:**
- No `Jira:` or `Jira Status:` lines in the file (the absence of `Jira:` is the marker that distinguishes the two).
- `/status` skips Jira sync for adhoc items (no `Jira:` line to sync); only local `Status:` exists.
- `/status` sweep moves adhoc items to `Complete\` only when local `Status:` = `Complete`.
- No automatic Jira transition during dev work.
- `Tier:` works identically to ticketed items — set during Triage.
- `/work <slug>` does NOT auto-scaffold for adhoc — the slug must already exist (created via `/adhoc`). Only a Jira-key argument auto-imports on miss; adhoc creation is a deliberate act via `/adhoc`.

## Communication

**Discussion entry format:** `[agent] message...`

Examples:
- `[Triage]` Tier=Standard: Two files, follows existing pattern. Logged tier.
- `[brainstorming]` Designed REST API approach with 3 endpoints. See spec.md.
- `[writing-plans]` Wrote plan.md with 5 tasks covering API + tests.
- `[csharp-developer]` Completed implementation, 4 files modified...
- `[orchestrator]` Answered: Use existing ValidationService pattern from UserController

**Add comments when:** Milestone complete, blocker found, status change, key decision

## Error Recovery

| Scenario | Recovery |
|----------|----------|
| Clone fails | Log to Discussion, set `Blocked: Clone failed: [error]` |
| Push rejected | `git pull --rebase origin fb/<KEY>-###`, resolve conflicts, retry |
| Agent crash mid-work | Next `/work` run detects Development status, resumes from last commit |
| Branch already exists | `git fetch origin fb/<KEY>-###` then `git checkout fb/<KEY>-###` |
| AgentWorkspace corrupt | Delete folder, re-clone |
